
{"id":"004079bb-5f1b-429c-97e7-7f7b17ecd080","object":"chat.completion","created":1784984039,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import json\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes', [])\\n    required_symbols = set(case.get('required_symbols', []))\\n\\n    if not nodes:\\n        raise ValueError('No nodes provided')\\n\\n    # Build dictionaries\\n    nodes_by_id = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    root_id = None\\n\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node.get('id')\\n        if not nid or not isinstance(nid, str):\\n            raise ValueError('Node must have a string id')\\n        if nid in nodes_by_id:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Duplicate node id: {nid}')\\n        nodes_by_id[nid] = node\\n        children_map[nid] = []\\n    \\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is None:\\n            if root_id is not None:\\n                raise ValueError('Multiple roots')\\n            root_id = node['id']\\n            if node.get('depth') != 0:\\n                raise ValueError('Root depth must be 0')\\n        else:\\n            if parent not in nodes_by_id:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Parent {parent} not found')\\n            children_map[parent].append(node['id'])\\n\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root node')\\n\\n    # Validate all nodes have required fields\\n    required_fields = ['id', 'parent', 'symbol', 'depth', 'removable', 'utility']\\n    for nid, node in nodes_by_id.items():\\n        for field in required_fields:\\n            if field not in node:\\n                raise ValueError(f'Node {nid} missing field {field}')\\n\\n    # Check required symbols initially present\\n    symbol_occurrences = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_occurrences[sym] = symbol_occurrences.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    missing_","reasoning_content":"We need to understand the task. The user provides a task description and some fixture data. The task is SE-PE-01: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". We need to implement solve(case) that takes a case payload (with nodes and required_symbols) and returns a JSON with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nFrom the description: we are reducing a parse tree by deleting removable nodes, but preserving required symbols and interestingness oracle (probably meaning we cannot remove nodes that cause required symbols to be missing). Also, deeper nodes before shallower nodes, stable tie break by node ID. We must not remove root, required symbol nodes, or nodes with retained children. Also, tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\n\nSo algorithm: We have a tree structure. Root is given with parent null. Each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Required symbols: list of strings (symbols that must be present in the retained tree). We need to decide which nodes to remove. Remove nodes that are removable, and not required symbol (by symbol? or by node? Required symbols are symbols, not node IDs. So we need to check if after removal, all required symbols are still present in the retained tree. Also can't remove root. Also can't remove nodes with retained children: if a node is removed, its children would become orphans? Actually in tree reduction, removing a node typically means its children get attached to its parent? Or maybe we just delete node and its subtree? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" - but we are only doing deletion? The problem says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" This suggests we can only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose children are also removed? \"a node with retained children\" means if any child is retained, we cannot remove the parent. So we can only remove a node if all its children are also removed (or it has no children). Also we must preserve required symbols: after removal, the set of symbols of retained nodes must still include all required symbols.\n\nAdditionally: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we should process nodes in order of descending depth. For same depth, tie break by node ID (string). So we need to sort nodes by depth descending, and for equal depth, by ID ascending? The description says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Usually ascending alphabetical order is stable. So we sort by (-depth, node_id). Then for each node in that order, if it's removable and not root and its symbol is not required? Actually we also cannot remove nodes that are required symbol? The required_symbols are symbols, not nodes. If a node's symbol is in required_symbols, we should not remove it because that would lose the required symbol. Unless there is another node with the same symbol? But required_symbols says \"all required symbols remain represented\" - if there are multiple nodes with same symbol, removing one might be okay if another remains. However the rule \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might mean a node whose symbol is required? Or maybe it means a node that is itself marked as required? The payload has \"removable\" field, and required_symbols is separate. So we need to interpret: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The required symbol likely refers to nodes that have a symbol in required_symbols? But it says \"a required symbol\", not \"a node with required symbol\". Could be ambiguous. The description also: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So if we remove a node, we must check that the set of symbols of remaining nodes still contains all required symbols. So if a required symbol appears only in that node, removal is not allowed. If it appears in multiple, removal might be allowed.\n\nAlso: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Probably means: do not remove: root node, any node that is a \"required symbol\" (maybe node that has symbol in required_symbols? But then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" would be redundant because if it is a required symbol node and the only one, removal would be blocked. But if there are multiple, could remove one? The second rule says never remove a required symbol, which is absolute. So I think the intended interpretation: The list required_symbols are symbols that must be present; each such symbol must be present at least once. However, the rule \"never remove a required symbol\" might be misinterpreted. Maybe it means \"never remove a node that is required\"? But there's no such field. The node has \"removable\" boolean. The root is not removable. So perhaps required_symbols means nodes that have that symbol are considered \"required\" and cannot be removed even if removable=True. But then the check \"all required symbols remain represented\" would be automatically true if no such node is removed. However the test in the sample: nodes have symbols like root, s1, s2, s0. required_symbols: ['root','s1']. In the sample, nodes with symbols 'root' are only the root (depth 0). So we cannot remove root anyway. Nodes with symbol 's1': there are multiple: n1 (depth1) and n4 (depth2) and maybe n6? n6 symbol is s0. So there are two s1 nodes. Could we remove one of them? The rule says never remove a required symbol - that would preclude removing any node with symbol 's1'. But that contradicts the \"tentatively remove if all required symbols remain\" logic. The candidate_artifact says \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" and \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\". Maybe we need to follow the rules exactly as described.\n\nLet's read the candidate_artifact more carefully: \"Implement `grammar_guided_node_reduction` through `solve(case)` for this bounded objective: Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle. Stay inside this mechanism boundary: Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded. Validate every required field before computing a result. Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes. Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order. Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties. Use only quantities present in the case payload. Do not call a language model, network service, random generator, wall clock, or environment-dependent resource. Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision, and compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation. Do not mutate the input. All ranking and traversal ties use the source position or identifier specified above; never depend on hash-map iteration. Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields. Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\"\n\nInterpretation: The objective is to reduce the tree by removing nodes, but we need to preserve \"interestingness\" which is defined by required_symbols? It says \"preserving an interestingness oracle\". That oracle likely checks whether required symbols are still present. So we must not remove nodes that cause loss of required symbols. Additionally, there is \"grammar_valid\" - meaning the tree must remain a valid tree (no orphans). If we remove a node, its children become children of its parent? In Perses, deletion means removing a node and connecting its children to its parent. But the problem statement says \"deletion and replacement candidates\". However, the rules: \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are retained, we cannot remove it. That suggests we can only remove nodes that have no children, or whose children are also being removed. In other words, we must remove from leaves upward. Because if we remove a parent, its children become orphan? Unless we also remove children. But the rule says we cannot remove a node with retained children. So we can only remove a node if all its children are also removed (or don't exist). So the algorithm: Process nodes in descending depth order. For each node, if it is removable and not root and (if we remove it, would all required symbols still be present after removal?) and also all its children are already removed (or it has no children), then we can remove it. But we need to track which nodes are removed and update the \"retained children\" condition. Since we process deepest first, children are processed before parents. So if a child is removed, it's no longer a retained child. So the condition \"node with retained children\" becomes: if any child is not removed, we cannot remove the parent. That matches processing order: at the time we consider a parent, its children have already been considered (since deeper). So we can check if any child is still present (not in removed set). That child is retained, so we cannot remove the parent.\n\nAlso, we need to ensure that after removal, the set of symbols of retained nodes contains all required_symbols. Since we remove nodes sequentially, we can check before removing each node: if the node's symbol is in required_symbols, then removing it might cause loss. But if there is another node with the same symbol still retained, it's okay. So we need to count occurrences of each required symbol among retained nodes. Before removal, we have current counts. If we remove this node, decrement count for its symbol. If any required symbol count goes to zero, cannot remove. This check should be done at each step.\n\nThe output: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in order of removal (first removed first?). Actually \"the actual removal order\" suggests that removed_node_ids should be ordered as they were removed. And retained_node_ids should be sorted (alphabetically? or by some order? It says \"sorted retained IDs\", likely sorted ascending by node ID string. Also removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed. Since we process nodes in depth descending order and tie break by node ID, that order will be the removal order (if we remove them as we go). But note: we might not remove all candidate nodes; some might be skipped because of constraints. The removal order is the order in which nodes are actually removed. So we should accumulate removed_node_ids in the order we remove them.\n\nEdge cases: Node depth is given. The root has parent null. We must validate that input has a root with parent null and depth 0, and ensure no cycles. The candidate_artifact says validate required fields. We can assume input is well-formed as per fixtures, but we should still check for sanity (e.g., all nodes have id, parent, etc.). If invalid, raise ValueError.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n- Parse case: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n- Build dictionary mapping node id to node object.\n- Build adjacency: for each node, store list of child ids (by scanning all nodes and adding child id to parent's list).\n- Identify root: node with parent null.\n- If not exactly one root, or root depth !=0, raise ValueError.\n- Check that all required symbols are present in at least one node initially. If not, raise ValueError (infeasible).\n- Initialize sets: removed = set(), retained = set(all node ids). Actually we start with all nodes retained. We'll process removal candidates.\n- Sort all node ids by depth descending, then by node id ascending (since we need stable tie break). Use node['depth'].\n- For each node id in that order:\n    node = nodes[id]\n    if node['id'] == root_id: skip (cannot remove root)\n    if not node['removable']: skip\n    if any child of this node is not in removed set: skip (has retained children)\n    # Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol count to become zero.\n    # Compute current symbol counts for required symbols among retained nodes.\n    # But we can also maintain counts incrementally. Initially count = number of nodes with that symbol in retained set.\n    # For each removal, if node's symbol in required_symbols, decrement count, if becomes 0, revert and skip.\n    # However, we need to also consider that this node might have children that are already removed, so their symbols are already gone. That's fine.\n    # So maintain dict: symbol_counts = Counter of symbols of retained nodes. Initially compute.\n    # For each candidate removal:\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        new_counts = symbol_counts.copy()\n        new_counts[sym] -= 1\n        if any(required_sym not in new_counts or new_counts[required_sym] == 0 for required_sym in required_symbols): skip\n        else:\n            # Remove node\n            removed.add(node_id)\n            retained.discard(node_id) # but we don't need retained set if we maintain symbol_counts based on retained nodes.\n            # Actually retained set is used for checking children (if child not in removed, it's retained). We'll keep removed set.\n            symbol_counts = new_counts\n            removed_node_ids.append(node_id)\n- After processing all, retained_node_ids = sorted([nid for nid in nodes if nid not in removed], key=lambda x: x) (or simply sort keys of nodes not in removed).\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nBut we need to ensure that we consider the \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\". The above seems to preserve interestingness (required symbols). Grammar valid: tree must remain connected? If we remove nodes but not their children? The rule \"never remove a node with retained children\" ensures that if we remove a node, all its children are already removed, so the tree remains connected (the children are gone). If a node is removed and its children are also removed, the parent's link to that node is broken but no orphan children. So tree remains a tree (minus removed subtrees). That should be grammar valid.\n\nOne nuance: The rule \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We are doing that by checking counts.\n\nAlso, the rule \"Never remove ... a required symbol.\" Might be interpreted as \"never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols\". But if we allow removal if there are duplicates, the rule \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" would allow it. The phrase \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" could be a stronger statement meaning \"do not remove any node that has a required symbol, period.\" But that would conflict with the other rule if duplicates exist. However, the example fixtures might clarify: In the sample, required_symbols: ['root','s1']. Node 'n1' has symbol 's1' and removable=false. So it won't be removed anyway. Node 'n4' has symbol 's1' and removable=true. If the rule says never remove a required symbol, then n4 cannot be removed even though there is another s1 node (n1). But would that be consistent with the objective of reducing the tree? Possibly yes. But the other rule says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That would allow n4 removal if n1 remains. So there is inconsistency. Which one to follow? The candidate_artifact lists: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Then separately: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" The double mention suggests they are separate constraints: the first is absolute (never remove root, never remove any node that is a required symbol (i.e., whose symbol is in required_symbols?), never remove node with retained children). The second is a softer check: even if node is not a required symbol, you still need to ensure all required symbols remain represented after removal. But if we interpret \"required symbol\" as a node that has a symbol in required_symbols, then the second check is unnecessary for those nodes because they can't be removed anyway. However, the second check might apply to nodes that are not required symbols but their removal might cause a required symbol to be absent (e.g., if a required symbol is only on a node that is a descendant, but removing a parent does not remove the descendant if we don't remove children? Actually removing a node with retained children is forbidden, so you can't remove a parent if child is retained. So the only way a required symbol could disappear is if the node itself has that symbol and is removed, or if its subtree contains the symbol and we remove the node along with its children. But if we remove a node and its children (since all children must be removed first), then we remove the entire subtree. So all symbols in that subtree are lost. So the second check is crucial: before removing a node, we must ensure that no required symbol is exclusively present in its subtree. Since we have already removed its children, those symbols are already gone? Wait, we process depth descending. If we remove a child first, the child's symbol is already removed from the retained set. So if that child's symbol was the only occurrence of a required symbol, removal would have been blocked at the child stage. So if we reach the parent, all children are already removed, meaning any required symbols that were only in the subtree are already gone. But we should not have allowed that child removal if it caused a required symbol to be lost. So by the time we consider the parent, the subtree's unique required symbols are already lost? That can't happen because we would have blocked child removal. So the parent removal might remove additional nodes that had different symbols. But if all children are already removed, the only node being removed now is the parent itself. So removing the parent only loses its own symbol. So we need to check that the parent's symbol is not required and uniquely present. So the second check for parent removal is just about its own symbol. But for child removal, we already did the check. So our algorithm that checks counts before each removal works.\n\nGiven the strict language \"Never remove a required symbol\", I think the intended interpretation is that a node whose symbol is in required_symbols is considered \"required\" and cannot be removed, regardless of duplicates. Because the phrase \"a required symbol\" is a noun phrase referring to the node that is a required symbol. In the context of parse trees, a \"symbol\" is like a non-terminal. The required_symbols list are the \"interesting\" non-terminals. The nodes are instances of symbols. So a node that has a symbol in required_symbols is a \"required symbol node\". And the rule says never remove such a node. This is a hard constraint. Then the second rule (Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented) might be a softer check for nodes that are not required symbols but whose removal could affect the presence of required symbols indirectly (like removing a node that is an ancestor of a required symbol). But as argued, you can't remove ancestor if children (including the required symbol node) are retained. So the second rule might be redundant if we enforce the first rule. However, in a tree reduction algorithm like Perses, there is often a concept of \"interestingness\" that is checked after each removal attempt. The candidate_artifact mentions \"interestingness_oracle\". The required_symbols are the interesting symbols. The oracle likely returns true if all required symbols are present. So the first rule (never remove a required symbol) is an optimization: you don't even attempt to remove those nodes because the oracle would reject it (if it's the only occurrence). But if there are duplicates, the oracle might allow, but the first rule forbids it absolutely. So we must follow the first rule as a hard constraint.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I need to look at the sample data and expected output? The user didn't provide expected outputs. However, the fixture includes multiple cases. The output from a correct implementation would be deterministic. Let's simulate both interpretations on the first case.\n\nCase 0: nodes: root (depth0, removable=false, symbol root), n1 (depth1, removable=false, symbol s1), n2 (depth1, removable=true, symbol s2), n3 (depth1, removable=false, symbol s0), n4 (depth2, removable=true, symbol s1), n5 (depth2, removable=false, symbol s2), n6 (depth2, removable=true, symbol s0). required_symbols: ['root','s1'].\n\nInterpretation A (allow removal of nodes with required symbol if duplicates exist):\nSort by depth (desc): depth2: n4, n5, n6 (tie break id: n4, n5, n6). Then depth1: n1, n2, n3. Then root.\n- Process n4: removable=true, children? n4 no children (depth2 leaf). symbol s1 (required). root and n1 also have s1? root symbol root, n1 symbol s1. So there are at least two s1 nodes (n1 and n4). If remove n4, counts for s1 goes from 2 to 1, still present. Check retained children: none, so can remove. So remove n4.\n- Process n5: removable=false, skip.\n- Process n6: removable=true, symbol s0 (not required). No children. Remove n6? But need to check required symbols remain: s1 still present (n1), root present. So remove n6.\n- Process n1: removable=false, skip.\n- Process n2: removable=true, symbol s2 (not required). Children: n4 (already removed). So no retained children. Remove n2? Required symbols still present (root, s1 from n1). So remove n2.\n- Process n3: removable=false, skip.\n- Root: not removable.\nResult: removed = [n4, n6, n2]; retained = [root, n1, n3, n5] (sorted: n1,n3,n5,root). But note n5 is retained (symbol s2, not required). That seems plausible.\n\nInterpretation B (never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols, i.e., n4 cannot be removed because symbol s1 is required):\nThen n4 is skipped. n6 removed (s0). n2 removed (s2). n1 not removable. n3 not removable. root not removable. So removed = [n6, n2]; retained = [root, n1, n3, n4, n5].\n\nWhich one is intended? The candidate_artifact explicitly says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The phrase \"a required symbol\" suggests nodes that are themselves symbols that are required. In parsing, a non-terminal is a symbol. The required_symbols list enumerates the interesting non-terminals. So a node that has that symbol is a required symbol. So I interpret it as absolute.\n\nBut then the rule \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" For nodes that are not required symbols, we still need to ensure that after removal, each required symbol appears at least once. For example, if we remove n2 (symbol s2) but its subtree might have contained a required symbol? But n2's children: n4 (s1) is a required symbol. However, we cannot remove n2 because it has a retained child (n4) initially. But after we process depth2 first, we might remove n4? But if we cannot remove n4 because it's a required symbol, then n4 remains, so n2 has a retained child, so we cannot remove n2. So n2 stays. So the algorithm would remove only n6, maybe. But then retained includes n2, n4, n5. That might be the expected reduction? Let's see the order: depth2 nodes: n4 (cannot remove because required symbol, so skip), n5 (removable false), n6 (removable true, no children, symbol s0 not required, so remove n6). Then depth1: n1 (removable false), n2 (removable true, has child n4 retained -> has retained children, so cannot remove). n3 (removable false). root skip. So removed = [n6]; retained = all others. That seems very minimal reduction. But the task might expect more reduction. The candidate_artifact mentions \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\" which aims to minimize the tree while preserving interestingness. Typically Perses will try to remove as many nodes as possible. The rule \"never remove a required symbol\" might be too strong if duplicates exist, because you could remove one duplicate. In Perses, you can remove any node as long as the interestingness oracle (the test) still passes. The oracle would check if required symbols are present. So if there are duplicates, removing one is fine. The statement \"never remove a required symbol\" could be interpreted as \"never remove a node that is necessary for the required symbols to be present\", but that's circular. The phrasing is ambiguous.\n\nLet's search online memory: The problem might be from a known benchmark? SE-PE-01 likely from \"effectslice\" or similar. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". The candidate_artifact says \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\". Perses is a tool for test-case reduction that uses a grammar. It works on parse trees and removes nodes if the test still passes (interestingness). The test is typically a script. Here, interestingness is defined by presence of required symbols. So we are simulating that reduction. In Perses, you can remove any node, including non-terminals that are required, as long as the test still passes (i.e., required symbols still appear). So the \"never remove a required symbol\" might be a mis-statement. Actually, Perses distinguishes between \"removable\" nodes (those that are optional in the grammar) and \"non-removable\" nodes (those that are required). The fixture has \"removable\" boolean. So nodes with removable=false cannot be removed. That's already enforced. The rule \"never remove a required symbol\" might refer to symbols that are required by the grammar (like root). But the required_symbols list is separate. I think the intended meaning is: never remove nodes that are marked as required by the grammar (i.e., the root must always be present, and any node that is required by the grammar cannot be removed). But in the fixture, the removable field indicates whether a node can be removed. So we already respect that. Then the rule about required symbols is about the interestingness oracle: after removals, all required symbols must still be present. So I think interpretation A (allow removal of nodes with required symbol if duplicates exist) is more consistent with Perses.\n\nBut the explicit phrase \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" is hard to ignore. Could it be that \"required symbol\" refers to symbols in the `required_symbols` list? And the argument is that if you remove a node with that symbol, you might lose that symbol, but the second rule says you can't if it would cause loss. So the first rule is redundant? No, because if there are duplicates, the first rule would forbid removal even if loss doesn't occur. That would be stricter.\n\nI should check the sample fixtures more: In all three fixtures, required_symbols are ['root','s1']. In each, there are two s1 nodes: one with removable=false (n1) and one with removable=true (n4). If we cannot remove n4 because it's a required symbol (s1), then we never remove any s1 node. That seems plausible. But then the root is always present. So the only removable nodes are n2 (s2) and n6 (s0). n4 is removable but cannot be removed because of symbol requirement. n2 has child n4 (retained) so cannot be removed. So only n6 can be removed. That would give removed: [n6] in all cases. That seems too trivial and not interesting. The task expects more complexity.\n\nConsider the second rule: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all r